    21	## 2. Summary verdict
    22	
    23	The honest-churn arm that round 3 asked for is done, and it is done well. It follows its plan, the pod gate is bit-identical, and every value in Table 12 and in the Section 9 paragraph reproduces from the records. The result is the most important one in the paper: at p = 0.05 the measured gain against R3 rises from 0.065 to 0.272, R3 becomes worse than C* by 0.173 and worse than B3 by 0.033 to 0.052, and at p = 0.01 nothing clear changes. But the paper does not yet use this result. The abstract, the model section and the limits still say that honest churn "is untested", the conclusion does not mention it, and the first and fourth findings in the abstract are stated without the condition that the new arm shows they need. The paragraph's explanation is also wrong in one part: on the three markets that carry the reversal, the winning attack never replaces its identity. The attacker does not hide among newcomers. It stays old, and R3 has no audits left for old identities, because honest newcomers hold 0.355 of the jobs and take 0.808 of all audits (my computation from the diagnostics). The two rates lie on the two sides of this saturation point, and the paper has no rate between them. All of this can be fixed with text and with analysis on existing records. Scores: **WEIS 2027: 3/5 (lower end; 35%). AAMAS main track: 2/5 (12%). ACM EC: 1/5 (3%).** The WEIS score is 3 and not 2 because the missing experiment now exists and its result is clear; it is at the lower end because the manuscript still argues around its own strongest result and is 33 pages.
