    95	
    96	### (c) Interpretation
    97	
    98	**Supported.**
    99	
   100	- "The rise comes from the attack's profit, not from a weaker reference" (pp. 16 to 17). The attack term is +113.1 and the reference term is -0.15, and reselecting the reference among the frozen menu under churn changes a training gain by at most 0.0046. Add the unit and the reselection value.
   101	- The audit-share diagnostic and expectation 4. The plan permits the statement that R3 exposes honest newcomers to more audits.
   102	- The exploratory label, the "illustrative, not calibrated" label, and the sentence that the model "does not isolate age information".
   103	
   104	**Not supported as written.**
   105	
   106	1. **"while the attacker's identity hides among them" (p. 17) is a category error.** The files say:
   107	   - The frozen R3 winner at p = 0.05 has the replacement switch off (`x = 0`) on fresh1, fresh2 and fresh4. Its newest share there is exactly 0.000 (`diagnostics.json` `knobs` and `focal_newest_by_first_offer_assigned`).
   108	   - These three markets carry the reversal: C* minus R3 is -0.446, -0.089 and -0.213 there, against -0.016, -0.270 and -0.006 on the others.
   109	   - Pooled, the attacker's jobs are audited at 0.042 under R3 (0.012 to 0.095 by market), against 0.292 under C* and 0.251 under B3 (my sums of the `focal` counts over all age bins).
   110	   - Under R3, honest identities younger than ten rounds hold 0.355 of the served jobs and receive 0.808 of all audits. Established honest jobs are audited at 0.078. The attacker holds 0.088 of the jobs and receives 0.015 of the audits (my computation, `sat.py`).
   111	   - My reading: the attacker is not one newcomer among many. It stops being a newcomer. R3's priority phase spends the quota on honest newcomers, and an identity that stays old is almost never audited. The winning attack is then plain shading without replacement, which is a D2 policy. This is the overload case of Lemma 2 (p. 7), and the first test already named it for entrant coalitions: "coalitions of entrants overload the newcomer priority" (p. 10).
   112	2. **"The diagnostics show how" (p. 17) gives the newest share a causal role that the design cannot give it.** The three values (0.010, 0.107, 0.452) come from three different selected attacks. The B3 winner replaces often because a ban costs it nothing; that is why it is often the newest. There is no no-churn value of this statistic in the paper. The plan itself says "A failure is not read as evidence of a particular cause". Report the shares "under each rule's selected attack" and move the weight to the audit shares, which are properties of the rule.
   113	3. **"the B3 minus R3 gap stays at 0.175" (p. 17) holds for the frozen searches only.** With the doubled searches the gap falls from 0.170 to 0.153 and g(R3) rises from 0.067 to 0.086. Table 12 shows this; the sentence does not.
   114	4. **The frozen row at p = 0.01 is visibly under-searched.** The frozen R3 winners on fresh1 and fresh3 have `x = 0` and gains 0.036 and 0.056; the doubled winners have `x = 1` and gains 0.130 and 0.082. The frozen C* winner on fresh1 has a gain of -0.000; at p = 0 it is 0.149 and at p = 0.05 it is 0.134. The pooled C* minus R3 value at p = 0.01 is set by fresh1 alone: -0.036 frozen and -0.075 doubled there, and positive on the other five markets with the doubled searches (0.003 to 0.014). Both fresh1 cells are flagged, each with a change of 0.053. The paper's caveat ("changes sign under doubling") is correct but too weak. The supported statement for p = 0.01 is: no reversal against B3 on any market; the C* contrast is not resolved by these searches.
   115	5. **The frozen g(B3) of 0.220 at p = 0.05 is one failed search.** The frozen fresh4 winner against B3 has a gain of 0.094; the doubled one has 0.196, and the no-churn value is 0.189. This is the largest flag in the arm (0.087). So "from 0.238 to 0.220" is not an effect of churn, and the frozen gap of -0.052 is too large. With the doubled searches it is -0.033; with the better of the two winners per cell it is -0.036, negative on 6 of 6 (my computation; this envelope is post hoc).
   116	6. **The closing sentence needs three conditions.** Quote (p. 17): "Within this model, at the higher rate, R3's measured advantage over C* and B3 does not survive honest churn."
   117	   - It is true for the pooled point estimates and for the seed intervals.
   118	   - The paper applies a market-level t interval to the D2 gap and to Q1, but not here. Here it gives [-0.352, 0.005] frozen and [-0.339, 0.001] doubled for C* minus R3, and [-0.119, 0.015] frozen and [-0.064, -0.001] doubled for B3 minus R3 (my computation, t with 5 degrees of freedom). The intervals touch zero because the market values are very unequal (-0.006 to -0.446), not because the sign varies. Report them with the sign counts (6 of 6 frozen, 5 of 6 doubled).
   119	   - For C* the sentence is too weak. A difference of -0.173 is more than three times the paper's own materiality margin of 0.05, in C*'s favour.
   120	   - For B3 it is about right: R3 is not better than B3 and may be slightly worse.
   121	   - It holds past the point where honest newcomers' jobs exceed the audit quota (new problem 3), and for frozen reference candidates.
   122	
   123	### (d) Contribution and placement
   124	
   125	It changes the main claim. This is no longer a limit; it is a result.
   126	
   127	- **Finding (i) becomes conditional.** Without honest churn, R3 and C* are equivalent within 0.05 with R3 lower. At p = 0.05, C* is lower by 0.173, outside the margin on the other side. C*'s own gain moves little (0.086 to 0.098) and its gross efficiency under attack does not move (0.993 at every rate; my computation from `eff_attack`).
   128	- **The fourth finding becomes conditional.** "R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market" is false at p = 0.05 on 5 or 6 of 6 markets.
   129	- **The third finding gets its population version.** The registered diagnostic had one honest replacement. Now, with 90 per episode, R3 audits 0.571 of honest newcomers' jobs and they absorb 0.808 of the audits.
   130	- **The reset and churn follow-ups give one consistent account** (my reasoning). R3's whole measured advantage is in the replacement channel, and it exists only while the attacker is almost the only newcomer. A rule that ranks by verified history does not depend on that. This is the paper's best contribution, and it agrees with the theory the paper cites on cheap pseudonyms (its references [13] and [11]).
   131	- **Placement.** It must be in the abstract, the contributions, "Results in brief", Section 10 and the conclusion. It should be a subsection of its own, not the third paragraph inside Q4.
   132	- **Wording for the abstract** (replaces "so honest churn is untested"): "An exploratory follow-up lets each honest opponent replace its identity with probability p per round. At p = 0.01 the comparison with B3 is unchanged and the comparison with C* is not resolved by the searches. At p = 0.05, where honest newcomers' jobs exceed the audit quota, the measured gain against R3 rises from 0.065 to 0.272, above the gain against C* (0.098) and B3 (0.220 to 0.240) on 5 or 6 of the six markets: R3 spends 0.81 of its audits on honest newcomers, and an attacker that keeps its identity is audited on 0.04 of its jobs. The rates are illustrative, the references are the frozen ones, and intermediate rates are untested."
   133	- **Wording for the limits:** "R3's advantage is measured only when the attacker is almost the only source of new identities."
   134	- **Title.** "Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free" now promises more than the paper supports. A subtitle such as "... and Why Age-Ranked Audits Fail Under Honest Churn" is the honest version (my judgement).
   135	
   136	## 5. New problems, ranked
   137	
   138	**1. The paper contradicts its own churn follow-up. (Text only.)**
   139	- Quote (abstract, p. 1): "honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested".
   140	- Quote (p. 5): "Population churn among honest opponents is untested, except for the single replacement of the second test's honest-replacement diagnostic (Section 10)."
   141	- Quote (p. 27): "population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power".
   142	- Quote (p. 7): "with honest churn it would share the newest positions with honest newcomers."
   143	- Quote (p. 27): "the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market".
   144	- The conclusion (p. 28) does not mention churn. The opening of Section 9 (p. 12) lists the exploratory tables without Tables 11 and 12.
   145	- This is round-3 problem 1 again, for the new arm.
   146	- Fix: replace all five sentences with the measured result and its conditions (wording in 4d). Add "without honest churn" to the first and fourth findings of the abstract, to contribution (i), and to the first sentence of Section 10. Add one sentence to the conclusion.
   147	
   148	**2. The stated mechanism is wrong. (Analysis on existing records, then text.)**
   149	- Quote (p. 17): "R3's newcomer priority thus moves audits to honest newcomers while the attacker's identity hides among them."
   150	- Evidence in 4c, point 1.
   151	- Fix: replace the sentence with: "R3's priority phase spends the quota on honest newcomers (0.355 of the served jobs, 0.808 of the audits), so established identities are almost never audited (0.078 of honest established jobs; 0.042 of the attacker's jobs, against 0.251 under B3). On three of the six markets the selected attack does not replace its identity at all." Add to Table 12, or to a new table, per rule and rate: the attacker's audited share, the share of audits that go to honest newcomers, and the number of winners with `x = 0`. All are in `diagnostics.json`.
   152	
   153	**3. The two rates lie on the two sides of a saturation point, and the paper does not say so. (Text only for the statement; needs compute for the rates between.)**
   154	- The share of honest identities younger than L = 10 rounds is 1 - (1 - p)^10: 0.096 at p = 0.01 and 0.401 at p = 0.05 (my arithmetic). The measured job shares are 0.085 and 0.355.
   155	- The audit ratio is 0.25 (audits over served jobs in every churn cell; `sat.py`).
   156	- At p = 0.01 the priority phase fits in the quota (R3 audits 0.880 of honest newcomers' jobs). At p = 0.05 it does not (0.571). The crossing is near p = 0.028 for these parameters (my arithmetic, ignoring capacity differences and the terminal reserve).
   157	- So "does not survive honest churn" is a statement about one regime: honest newcomer volume above the audit quota. It depends on p, L, the audit ratio and the honest share of capacity, all fixed here.
   158	- Fix (text): state the condition and the arithmetic beside the result, and say that the range between 0.01 and 0.05 is untested. Fix (compute): run p = 0.02 and p = 0.03 for the three rules. By the ledger, two rates cost about 5 USD.
   159	
   160	**4. Efficiency under churn is in the records and not in the paper. (Analysis on existing records.)**
   161	- Pooled gross efficiency under the selected attack, frozen searches (`eff_attack`, my computation): R3 0.994 (p = 0), 0.989 (0.01), 0.967 (0.05). C* 0.993, 0.993, 0.993. B3 0.959, 0.959, 0.940.
   162	- On fresh2 at p = 0.05, R3's value is 0.893. The registered Q4 efficiency threshold is 0.90 (Table 3). This is a point estimate in an exploratory arm, not a registered failure.
   163	- R3's gain at p = 0.05 is 0.580 on fresh1 and 0.365 on fresh3. The first test's absolute ceiling for R3's gain was 0.30 (p. 9).
   164	- Fix: add efficiency columns to Table 12 and one sentence. State that the registered efficiency label "condition met" is also a no-churn result.
   165	
   166	**5. The p = 0.05 result lacks the qualifications the paper gives elsewhere. (Analysis on existing records.)**
   167	- Doubled searches: 5 of 6 markets, not 6 (fresh0 has the other sign for both contrasts).
   168	- Market-level t intervals are missing (values in 4c, point 6).
   169	- The frozen B3 value depends on one flagged cell (4c, point 5).
   170	- Fix: print the per-market d and q for both versions (as the lower block of Table 11 does), the sign counts for both versions, and the t intervals. Replace "from 0.238 to 0.220" with "0.238 to 0.220 with the frozen searches (one flagged cell, fresh4) and 0.237 to 0.240 with the doubled ones".
   171	
   172	**6. The p = 0.01 sentences read more from the frozen row than it can carry. (Text only; Run B would settle it.)**
   173	- Evidence in 4c, points 3 and 4.
   174	- Fix: "At p = 0.01 the B3 minus R3 gap is 0.175 with the frozen searches and 0.153 with the doubled ones, positive on every market. The C* minus R3 difference is 0.001 and -0.004; its sign is set by fresh1, where both cells are flagged (change 0.053), so it is not resolved." Add that 7 of the 18 cells at p = 0.01 are flagged.
   175	
   176	**7. The abstract's first finding and its "direction R3 lower" label are now conditional, and the paper does not connect them to the churn result. (Text only.)**
   177	- Quote (abstract): "R3 and C* are 'equivalent, direction R3 lower' within a pre-set margin of ±0.05".
   178	- The registered label stays. But a reader must learn in the same place that the direction reverses by 0.173 at p = 0.05.
   179	- The comparator caveat ("selected in development on gross efficiency in effect") now cuts the other way: a comparator that was not chosen for attacker gain is the one that is robust to churn. Whether that holds for another credibility rule is not known (see Run A).
   180	
   181	**8. Reporting gaps against the plan. (Text only.)**
   182	- Print the reselection diagnostic (16 of 36 nonzero, largest 0.0046) and the largest flag change (0.087).
   183	- Add "raw profit units" to "rises by 113.101", or give 0.209 in gain units.
   184	- Say that the timing check showed the old attack's profit rising with p.
   185	- Say that the reference candidates are frozen and that the menus were not regenerated under churn (the plan says it; the paper says only "frozen truthful reference played under the same churn").
   186	
   187	**9. Process records. (Text only.)**
   188	- The ledger names no plan commit for the churn arm. Add ef17139 (11:45:42 UTC) and the pod request time (11:46).
   189	- `SECOND_REVIEW_TRIAGE.md` reports a field rename that the package does not contain (section 3, row 5).
   190	- The reset reproduction command needs `scipy`; say so, or remove the dependency.
   191	- The paper states an authorisation of 55 USD (p. 12). The ledger gives a program total of 57.51 USD under a limit of 123 USD after the follow-ups. A search of the paper text for "USD" finds only the 12, 40, 43, 52 and 55 USD statements (pp. 10 and 12); the follow-up spend (1.65 and 4.99 USD by the ledger) is not stated. Add it.
   192	
   193	**10. Length. (Text only.)**
   194	- 33 pages. Each round adds pages. With the churn result the paper has a clear centre; see section 7.
   195	
   196	## 6. Open runs, ranked
   197	
   198	Neither approved run must be done before a WEIS submission. The text fixes of problems 1 to 6 must be.
   199	
   200	**1. Run A, the runner-up comparator. It changes my score more, if it includes the churn cells.**
   201	- As approved (six fresh markets, no churn), it tests the "direction R3 lower" label. That label matters less now.
   202	- Extended with p = 0.05 (and p = 0.01), it answers the question the churn arm opened: is robustness to honest churn a property of verified-history targeting, or of one configuration, C-mem(6, 20)?
   203	- If C-thr(12) is also robust, the paper's main claim becomes general across the comparator family, and WEIS moves to a firm 3 or to 4. If it is not, the claim stays about one rule, and the paper must say so.
   204	- Recommended before WEIS. Not required, if the abstract keeps the comparator caveat.
   205	
   206	**2. Run B, continued searches. It changes the p = 0.01 statements and little else.**
   207	- Extend it to the 13 flagged churn cells. The cells that matter are fresh1 (R3 and C*, p = 0.01) and fresh4 (B3, p = 0.05).
   208	- It cannot change the p = 0.05 result against C*: the gap there is 0.17, and the largest flag is 0.087 on a B3 cell.
   209	- Required for AAMAS. Not required for WEIS.
